From c7eacf261d7d1812259fb9636b156fd59d589dd9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Kimathi <kimathi@example.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 14:17:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add self-service account deletion (soft delete)

New: POST /api/v1/auth/account/delete/ (requires current password).

This is a soft delete - the account is deactivated (is_active=False,
all outstanding refresh tokens blacklisted) but the User row and all
linked academic records (enrollments, timetable history, uploads,
notifications) are kept intact for the institution's records, per
Student.user and TimetableSlot.lecturer both being PROTECTed FKs -
a hard delete would either need to relax those safeguards or fail
outright for any account with real history.

If the user is a lecturer, they're automatically unassigned from
every timetable slot they're on (lecturer set to null) as part of
deletion, so a deactivated lecturer doesn't keep showing as teaching
a live class.

Deactivation takes effect immediately, not just at token expiry:
Django's authenticate() already blocks inactive users at login, and
SimpleJWT's JWTAuthentication.get_user() checks is_active on every
request, so an already-issued access token stops working the moment
is_active flips - the refresh-token blacklisting is additional
defense in depth on top of that, not the only thing making this work.

Also fixed two more real, pre-existing bugs in LecturerRegisterView
found while building a real end-to-end test for this (register a
lecturer -> assign them to a slot -> delete -> verify): it referenced
an undefined UserSerializer (never imported, though the class exists
at apps/accounts/serializers/user_serializer.py) and passed a 'title'
kwarg to Lecturer.objects.create() that doesn't exist on the model
(the real field is 'rank') - lecturer registration was completely
broken in production before this.

Verified end-to-end: registered a lecturer, assigned them to a live
timetable slot, logged in, then deleted the account - confirmed
password validation (missing/wrong password both correctly
rejected), the slot was unassigned, the same still-unexpired access
token immediately stopped working, login was blocked, and the
Lecturer profile + timetable slot record were both preserved
untouched.
